A modern enterprise is a new automated production system capable of meeting the ever-changing needs of people. This is a real industrial revolution in production. At the current stage, accelerating scientific and technological progress is unimaginable without comprehensive automation of production. Automation and control of technological processes and production is a field of science and technology that includes a set of tools, methods, and approaches used for controlling moving objects, autonomous systems, technological processes, and production.
Optimizing the value chain in mining is not an easy task; the key to solving it is having complete information about all stages of raw material processing and product transportation. An integrated solution eliminates gaps in the supply chain and ensures reliable communication between production points, for example, coordinating blasting in a mine with grinding processes and the overall operation of an enrichment plant.
Automation in the chemical industry. Special attention is paid to integrated automation and mechanization of the chemical industry, as chemical processes are complex, high-speed, sensitive to deviations from set modes, and associated with harmful environments, explosion risks, and fire hazards of processed materials.
Challenges of automation in the chemical industry include lack of data on complex technological processes and difficulties in comparing available information to optimize performance.
Modern automation in the chemical industry is widely used to optimize important indicators such as personnel safety, environmental protection, and compliance with quality control standards. Implementation of automation leads to lower production costs and maximizes efficiency in consumer goods manufacturing.
Automation of energy systems. Automation of energy systems involves using devices with built-in programs and settings to control processes of production, conversion, and distribution of electricity and heat energy. Automated systems ensure efficient and reliable operation of power plants, substations, and transmission lines, as well as maintaining the required quality of electricity and heat.
In the Zarafshan valley, measures are being taken to develop the engineering industry, modernize and re-equip production facilities, and increase profitability and competitiveness of products. The main tasks of accelerating mechanical engineering production are:
– improving efficiency and quality of engineering products;
– accelerating innovative development of enterprises;
– increasing commercialization of local practical developments;
– accelerating fundamental research;
– directing the Zarafshan valley’s scientific-technical complex towards a full cycle of 'science–technology–production–consumer'.
Uzbekistan has created a strong mechanical engineering industry, which is the core of heavy industry. However, industries such as instrument-making, radio engineering, electronics, and automobile manufacturing remain underdeveloped, leading to a significant increase in imports of machinery and equipment.
